0,Nilo-Saharan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nilo-Saharan_languages,"The Nilo-Saharan languages are a proposed linguistic family consisting of around 210 Native African languages [1] spoken by somewhere around 70 million speakers, [1] mainly in the upper parts of the Chari and Nile rivers, including historic Nubia, north of where the two tributaries of the Nile meet. The languages extend through 17 nations in the northern half of Africa: from Algeria to Benin ...",Daatsʼíin,Nilo-Saharan
1,Sub-Saharan African music traditions - Wikipedia,https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sub-Saharan_African_music_traditions,"Drumming and dancing at Dakawa, Morogoro, Tanzania In many parts of sub-Saharan Africa, the use of music is not limited to entertainment: it serves a purpose to the local community and helps in the conduct of daily routines. Traditional African music supplies appropriate music and dance for work and for religious ceremonies of birth, naming, rites of passage, marriage and funerals. [1] The ...",Daatsʼíin,Nilo-Saharan
2,Sub Saharan African music traditions - Alchetron,https://alchetron.com/Sub-Saharan-African-music-traditions,"Sub-Saharan African music traditions exhibit so many common features that they may in some respects be thought of as constituting a single musical system. While some African music is clearly contemporary-popular music and some is art-music, still a great deal is communal and orally transmitted while still qualifying as a religious or courtly genre.",Daatsʼíin,Nilo-Saharan
3,Sub-Saharan African Music Traditions,https://www.scribd.com/document/402125153/Sub-Saharan-African-music-traditions-pdf-pdf,"Sub-Saharan African music traditions exhibit many common features and can be thought of as a single system. Music is integral to daily life and community, serving purposes beyond entertainment like work, rituals, and storytelling. The region is divided into seven ethnomusicological areas: West Africa, Central Africa, Eastern Africa, Northern Africa, the Sahel/Sudan region, and the Horn of ...",Daatsʼíin,Nilo-Saharan
